WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.818
India is one of the oldest countries in the world.

2
00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:07.564
This fascinating place has a diverse and colorful history.

3
00:00:07.565 --> 00:00:17.653
Almost one-fifth of the entire world’s population lives in India; it is the second most populous country on earth, after China!

4
00:00:17.654 --> 00:00:21.069
Its geography is as diverse as its people.

5
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:27.001
It has high mountains in the north, dry deserts in the west, and tropical beaches in the south.

6
00:00:27.002 --> 00:00:37.649
The people of India have adapted to life in all these different climates, and this is reflected in their culture, heritage, and language.

7
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.171
The people of India love to celebrate life.

8
00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:46.332
They are proud of their rich traditions and vibrant culture, and love to gather to celebrate any occasion.

9
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.786
This is why they have many different festivals.

10
00:00:48.787 --> 00:00:52.258
There are hundreds of festivals all across India every month.

11
00:00:52.259 --> 00:00:58.335
There are so many that there is a holiday for every day of the year.

12
00:00:58.336 --> 00:01:03.833
Some festivals last only a day, some one week, and some last a whole month!

13
00:01:03.834 --> 00:01:12.119
Most often, Indian people celebrate the changing of the seasons, the beginning of the harvest, the full moon, and the arrival of rain.

14
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:19.491
They also honor religious and national holidays such as the birthdays of saints, national heroes, and gurus.

15
00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:24.960
Each festival has a different name and is celebrated in a different way.

16
00:01:24.961 --> 00:01:28.087
Holi, the Festival of Colors

17
00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:32.275
One of the most popular festivals in India is the festival of Holi.

18
00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:36.971
This festival marks the end of winter and the beginning of the first harvest.

19
00:01:36.972 --> 00:01:41.384
It is usually celebrated at the end of February or the beginning of March.

20
00:01:41.385 --> 00:01:44.288
It starts on the night of the first full moon of spring.

21
00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:53.488
People begin to celebrate by lighting large bonfires in the middle of their towns to scare away evil spirits and ghosts.

22
00:01:53.489 --> 00:02:00.012
The following morning they run around the streets spraying each other with brightly colored powders and colored water.

23
00:02:00.013 --> 00:02:03.561
This is a time when people forget their worries and have fun.

24
00:02:03.562 --> 00:02:06.896
Everyone gets completely covered in a rainbow of colors.

25
00:02:06.897 --> 00:02:12.105
Around lunchtime the celebrations end, and people head to the river to wash off.

26
00:02:12.106 --> 00:02:16.464
They spend the rest of the day at home with their families.

27
00:02:16.465 --> 00:02:19.193
Diwali, the Festival of Lights

28
00:02:19.194 --> 00:02:23.341
Another popular festival is Diwali, or the festival of lights.

29
00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:28.794
All people─rich and poor, old and young, men and women─celebrate it.

30
00:02:28.795 --> 00:02:39.831
It is a four-day-long celebration, during which people decorate their homes with lanterns and set beautiful candles adrift on rivers or the ocean.

31
00:02:39.832 --> 00:02:46.173
By lighting lanterns, people thank their gods for giving them health, happiness, children, and knowledge.

32
00:02:46.174 --> 00:02:52.132
Diwali gives people a chance to scare away dark spirits and welcome light into their lives.

33
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:57.962
The festival ends with a beautiful fireworks display at night.

34
00:02:57.963 --> 00:03:01.719
Makar Sankranti, the Festival of Kites

35
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:10.804
A festival called Makar Sankranti celebrates the end of the long winter when the sun enters the northern hemisphere, which occurs on January 14.

36
00:03:10.805 --> 00:03:18.488
The sky fills up with hundreds of colorful shapes as people gather to fly kites, each one more beautiful than the next.

37
00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:24.437
People receive prizes for the most original kite or for flying their kite with the most skill.

38
00:03:24.438 --> 00:03:33.577
At night, people light lanterns, attach them to their kites, and fill the night sky with thousands of glowing lights.

39
00:03:33.578 --> 00:03:35.078
Gandhi Festival

40
00:03:35.079 --> 00:03:42.142
Each year on October 2, the people of India gather to celebrate the birth of their national hero, Mahatma Gandhi.

41
00:03:42.143 --> 00:03:45.847
Gandhi fought to free India from British rule without violence.

42
00:03:45.848 --> 00:03:50.231
He led peaceful marches throughout the country and united the people of India.

43
00:03:50.232 --> 00:03:58.937
On this national holiday, people visit the many statues of Gandhi all around India and sing Gandhi’s favorite songs.

44
00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.492
Yugadi, the Hindu New Year

45
00:04:01.493 --> 00:04:05.251
Yugadi is the New Year celebration in the Hindu calendar.

46
00:04:05.252 --> 00:04:08.768
Each year it falls on a different day between March and April.

47
00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:11.466
It is a day of forgiveness and new beginnings.

48
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:16.766
On the day of the holiday, people exchange sweets and ask each other for forgiveness.

49
00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:23.190
It is a day when merchants and accountants pay their debts as well.

50
00:04:23.191 --> 00:04:26.069
Dasara, the Festival of Good and Evil

51
00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:30.875
Dasara is a ten-day festival that celebrates the victory of good over evil.

52
00:04:30.876 --> 00:04:34.325
It is held in the town of Mysore in October.

53
00:04:34.326 --> 00:04:37.864
The whole town celebrates with concerts and dances.

54
00:04:37.865 --> 00:04:42.512
People decorate their homes with lanterns, candles, and flowers for nine days.

55
00:04:42.513 --> 00:04:50.090
On the tenth day, everyone dresses up in a colorful costumes and dances all around the town.

56
00:04:50.091 --> 00:04:57.578
This carnival is a rich celebration full of musicians, clowns, decorated elephants, and traditional dancers.

57
00:04:57.579 --> 00:05:03.583
The people of India are proud of their country’s heritage, and this pride shows in the way they celebrate life.

58
00:05:03.584 --> 00:05:10.902
By honoring their traditions with colorful festivals, they show their respect and appreciation for India’s rich and varied culture.

59
00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:19.903
Every day is a festival of a different kind, making India a very exciting place!

